const L=[];
L.push({id:'pio-entities',comp:'m1.c1',title:'Model entities around behavior and invariants',before:'Python Fundamentals covers functions, dictionaries and exceptions. Here a class coordinates state and the operations allowed on it.',intro:'A library loan has an identity, a borrowing member and a return state. A class is useful when related operations must preserve rules over that state, rather than merely group fields under a new name.',sections:[['Create instances with explicit state','A class defines a type and methods; calling it creates an instance whose initializer can establish valid state. Instance methods receive the instance as self by convention. Store each loan’s fields on that instance so unrelated loans do not share mutable state.','Choose methods that represent meaningful operations. Returning a loan should change only the permitted state and reject an invalid transition. The class does not automatically provide persistence, authentication or concurrency protection; those are separate service boundaries.'],['Preserve the invariant at mutation boundaries','An invariant is a condition that must remain true before and after supported operations. Validate construction and later updates rather than assuming every caller remembered the rule. A leading underscore communicates a nonpublic attribute by convention, but is not an authorization mechanism.','Keep a model small enough to test. If a class only carries values, a dataclass may fit; if it coordinates remote effects, consider separating those adapters from the local rule. The example protects one local return transition only.']],problem:'Give two synthetic loans independent state and prevent a repeated return.',steps:['Initialize the member and return state.','Apply the valid return once.','Observe that another instance and a repeat call are distinct.'],code:'class Loan:\n    def __init__(self, member):\n        self.member = member\n        self.returned = False\n    def mark_returned(self):\n        if self.returned:\n            raise ValueError("already returned")\n        self.returned = True\na, b = Loan("member-a"), Loan("member-b")\na.mark_returned()\nprint(a.returned, b.returned)\ntry:\n    a.mark_returned()\nexcept ValueError:\n    print("repeat rejected")',expect:'True False\nrepeat rejected',result:'The instances retain independent state and the repeated local transition is rejected. This is not a database transaction or cross-worker lock.',mistakes:['A class does not make arbitrary mutation safe automatically.','An underscore is a convention, not a security boundary.'],takeaways:['Model behavior around explicit state rules.','Test construction and mutation boundaries separately.'],refs:['classes','datamodel'],q:[
['E','What does self normally refer to in an instance method?','The current instance','Every instance at once','Only the parent module','The Python interpreter process','An instance method receives the object on which it operates as its first bound argument.','Instances retain separate identity.|The module is a different object.|The process is not the instance.'],
['E','Which initialization keeps a loan’s returned flag on that object?','self.returned = False','A shared global flag only','A new local with no self','The class name as text','An instance attribute stores per-object state reachable by later methods.','A global merges unrelated loans.|An unused local disappears after the call.|Text is not state storage.'],
['M','A loan may be returned only once. Where should the rule be checked?','At the state transition','Only in a button caption','Only in a comment','After declaring success','The mutation boundary must reject an invalid repeat under the model contract.','UI text cannot enforce the model.|Comments do not execute.|Success must follow validation.'],
['M','What is an invariant of a model?','A condition operations preserve','A random display preference','Every method’s line number','An automatically encrypted field','An invariant defines valid state across supported operations.','Display preferences need not be invariants.|Line numbers are incidental.|Model rules do not imply encryption.'],
['H','A class prevents repeated returns in one process. Which guarantee is still unsupported?','Concurrent database safety','Local method execution','A raised ValueError','Independent fixture instances','Cross-worker atomicity requires a real shared state boundary and corresponding tests.','The method ran locally.|The error is observed.|The two instances were created separately.'],
['H','What does naming an attribute _owner establish?','A nonpublic convention','Server authorization by itself','Unbreakable read protection','A database foreign key','The underscore communicates intended API use without imposing an access-control boundary.','Authorization needs real policy.|Python convention is not secrecy.|Names do not create constraints.'] ]});
L.push({id:'pio-attributes',comp:'m1.c2',title:'Distinguish class defaults from instance-owned values',before:'Understand construction and self from the entity lesson.',intro:'A support-ticket class needs a shared category label but a different tag list for each ticket. Class and instance attribute lookup make those two choices behave differently. A mutable value placed on the class can create a surprising shared state defect.',sections:[['Follow attribute lookup before assignment','Class attributes are shared definitions accessible through the class and, when not shadowed, through its instances. Assigning an ordinary attribute on one instance normally creates or replaces that instance’s own value; it does not change the class default for every other instance. Descriptors and properties can customize this behavior, so the simple model has a stated scope.','Mutating a shared object is different from assigning a new attribute. If tags=[] is defined on the class, append through an instance mutates that same shared list unless an instance-owned list has been established. Initialize mutable per-record collections inside __init__.'],['Use shared values deliberately','A stable category constant can live on the class when the design intends a shared default. Do not store a request’s account, tenant or temporary buffer there. Tests should create multiple instances and check for unintended cross-object contamination.','The fixture contrasts an instance shadowing a string default with independently initialized lists. It demonstrates ordinary attributes, not property descriptors, metaclasses or thread synchronization.']],problem:'Trace ordinary attribute shadowing while preserving separate tag lists.',steps:['Define a class-level priority default.','Give one instance its own priority and append its own tag.','Compare both instances with the unchanged class value.'],code:'class Ticket:\n    priority = "normal"\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.tags = []\na, b = Ticket(), Ticket()\na.priority = "urgent"\na.tags.append("billing")\nprint(a.priority, b.priority, Ticket.priority)\nprint(a.tags, b.tags)',expect:"urgent normal normal\n['billing'] []",result:'Only one instance shadows the priority and owns the billing tag. The shared class default remains normal.',mistakes:['A mutable class list can leak one record’s data into another.','Assigning an instance field and mutating a shared object are not equivalent.'],takeaways:['Initialize mutable per-instance collections explicitly.','Use class attributes only for deliberately shared state or defaults.'],refs:['classes','datamodel'],q:[
['E','Where should each ticket’s independent mutable tag list usually be created?','Inside __init__ on self','As one shared class list','In an unrelated global list','Only in a display string','Creating the list per initialization gives each instance separate storage.','A class list is shared.|A global list also shares state.|A string does not store the collection.'],
['E','An instance lacks its own ordinary priority attribute. Where can Python find the class default?','On its class','Only in a database','Only in another instance','Nowhere under any condition','Normal attribute lookup can use a class attribute when the instance does not shadow it.','A database is not required.|Other instances are not the default lookup source.|The class can supply the value.'],
['M','a.priority is assigned urgent while the class default is normal. What is the ordinary result for a new instance b?','b uses normal','b must use urgent','b has no class at all','b becomes the same object as a','Ordinary instance assignment shadows the default only for a.','The class default was not changed.|The class still exists.|Assignment does not merge identities.'],
['M','A class defines tags=[] and two instances append through that attribute. What can happen?','They mutate one shared list','Each append copies the class','The list is immutable','Python always rejects append','Both lookups can resolve to the same mutable class object.','Append does not copy classes.|Lists are mutable.|Append is valid on a list.'],
['H','Which test best exposes shared mutable instance data?','Two separate instances','One object printed twice','Only the class name','An import with no objects','Cross-instance contamination requires observing whether one object’s mutation affects another.','One instance cannot distinguish intended sharing.|Names do not execute state changes.|Import alone does not exercise the defect.'],
['H','A lesson about normal attributes excludes descriptors. Why should that limit be stated?','Descriptors can alter lookup','Descriptors encrypt all fields','Classes cannot have methods','Instances cannot store values','Properties and descriptors can customize access/assignment beyond the ordinary attribute model.','They are not automatic encryption.|Classes can define methods.|Instances can own attributes.'] ]});
L.push({id:'pio-dataclasses',comp:'m1.c3',title:'Use dataclasses and special methods as explicit contracts',before:'Understand instance state, class defaults and mutable collection ownership.',intro:'A shipment quote carries a route and a price in minor units. Dataclasses can generate repetitive initialization, representation and equality behavior, but those generated methods are still a design choice. Decide which fields define value equality and what information should appear in logs.',sections:[['Generate value-model mechanics deliberately','A dataclass uses annotated fields to generate methods such as __init__, __repr__ and, by default, equality. Equal values are not necessarily the same object. A field can be excluded from repr or comparison when its role justifies that choice, but hiding repr does not secure the underlying data.','For mutable defaults, use a default factory so each instance receives its own collection. frozen=True prevents ordinary field assignment through the dataclass API but does not recursively freeze mutable referenced objects. Type annotations do not validate runtime inputs by themselves.'],['Choose readable representation and meaningful equality','__str__ should produce a useful human-readable form while __repr__ supports unambiguous debugging where practical. Neither should reveal secrets. Equality semantics should match the domain: comparing two quote values may be useful, while entity identity may depend on a stable key rather than all transient fields.','The fixture uses an immutable-looking value model with integer fields and an explicit string method. It shows equality and identity separately. It does not validate currencies, enforce a persistence identity or demonstrate deep immutability.']],problem:'Compare equal quote values while showing that they remain distinct objects.',steps:['Declare annotated route and minor-unit amount fields.','Define a concise nonsecret string representation.','Compare value equality and object identity independently.'],code:'from dataclasses import dataclass\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass Quote:\n    route: str\n    amount_minor: int\n    def __str__(self):\n        return f"{self.route}: {self.amount_minor}"\na, b = Quote("A-B", 2500), Quote("A-B", 2500)\nprint(a == b, a is b)\nprint(str(a))',expect:'True False\nA-B: 2500',result:'The values compare equal while identity differs. Generated equality does not imply one shared object or automatic validation.',mistakes:['Type annotations in a dataclass do not enforce runtime types.','frozen=True does not deeply freeze every referenced collection.'],takeaways:['Choose equality and representation according to domain meaning.','Use default factories for mutable per-instance fields.'],refs:['dataclasses','datamodel'],q:[
['E','What does == normally use for a default dataclass value comparison?','Its generated equality','Only object identity','Only the variable names','A database lookup always','Dataclass equality can compare the declared fields under its configured rules.','Identity uses is.|Names are not value fields.|No database is required.'],
['E','How should a dataclass create independent list defaults?','Use a default factory','Reuse one global list','Store a list on the class','Use the same list argument forever','A factory creates a fresh collection for each instance initialization.','A global list shares state.|A class list is shared.|Reusing one object loses independence.'],
['M','Two quotes have equal field values but are separately constructed. Which result is possible?','Equal but not identical','Always identical objects','Different classes automatically','No equality is allowed','Value equality and object identity are separate questions.','Construction need not intern objects.|Equal fields do not change types.|Dataclasses can generate equality.'],
['M','A field is repr=False. What does that guarantee?','Omission from generated repr','Encryption in memory','Exclusion from all APIs','Authorization of every reader','The option controls generated representation only.','Representation is not encryption.|Other projections can still expose the field.|Access policy remains separate.'],
['H','A frozen dataclass contains a mutable list. What remains possible without further design?','Mutating the referenced list','Ordinary reassignment is required','Deep immutability is automatic','All references are encrypted','Frozen field assignment does not recursively freeze referenced objects.','The list can change without rebinding the field.|Deep immutability needs additional choices.|Freezing is not encryption.'],
['H','An entity’s equality should ignore a changing display label. What design question matters?','Which fields define identity','Only the class filename','Only the print color','Whether every field is public','Equality must reflect the domain’s identity/value contract, not accidental mutable presentation state.','Filenames do not define domain identity.|Color is unrelated.|Visibility alone does not choose equality.'] ]});
L.push({id:'pio-composition',comp:'m2.c1',title:'Prefer explicit composition when behavior varies independently',before:'Know classes and instance methods; no inheritance hierarchy is required for this example.',intro:'A receipt service can deliver by email or SMS while keeping the same receipt calculation. Inheriting every combination of receipt type and delivery channel creates an expanding hierarchy. Composition supplies a collaborator with the required operation instead.',sections:[['Separate is-a from has-a reasoning','Inheritance can express a substitutable specialization with a compatible contract. Composition places another object inside a service and delegates a responsibility. The phrase is-a is a starting question, not a proof that a subtype respects behavioral expectations.','A receipt service has a sender. The sender can change independently of receipt calculation, making composition a natural boundary. Pass the collaborator explicitly so tests can replace external delivery with a local recording implementation. This does not mean every inheritance relationship is wrong.'],['Keep the delegated contract small','Define what send receives, what it returns and how failures propagate. A collaborator that silently changes recipient meaning or invents success violates the interface even if its method has the right name. Use an adapter when an external provider’s contract differs from the application’s needs.','The fixture delegates a synthetic message to an in-memory sender and verifies the requested output. It does not send real mail or establish that a provider adapter works. A separate integration test must cover that boundary.']],problem:'Replace a delivery mechanism without subclassing the receipt service.',steps:['Define a local sender with one send operation.','Pass it into the service constructor.','Generate a receipt through delegation and inspect its outcome.'],code:'class MemorySender:\n    def send(self, recipient, text):\n        return f"queued for {recipient}: {text}"\nclass ReceiptService:\n    def __init__(self, sender): self.sender = sender\n    def receipt(self, recipient, amount):\n        return self.sender.send(recipient, f"amount={amount}")\nservice = ReceiptService(MemorySender())\nprint(service.receipt("synthetic-account", 1200))',expect:'queued for synthetic-account: amount=1200',result:'The service delegates through an explicit collaborator. No real delivery or receipt persistence occurred.',mistakes:['A method-name match alone does not guarantee substitutable behavior.','Subclassing every independent option can create a combinatorial hierarchy.'],takeaways:['Use composition for independently varying responsibilities.','State and test collaborator behavior, not only its name.'],refs:['classes','datamodel'],q:[
['E','A service stores a sender collaborator and delegates send. Which relationship is illustrated?','Composition','Integer inheritance','Object deletion','Automatic network delivery','The service is composed with another object that owns a distinct responsibility.','Integers are unrelated.|No object is being deleted.|The collaborator may be purely local.'],
['E','What makes a replacement sender useful to the service?','A compatible send contract','An identical source filename','The same display color','A longer class name','The service relies on behavior and failure semantics of the collaborator.','Names on disk do not define behavior.|Color is irrelevant.|Length does not establish compatibility.'],
['M','Receipt type and delivery channel vary independently. What avoids a subclass for every combination?','Separate collaborators','One giant subclass tree','A shared mutable global recipient','Duplicating every service method','Composition can separate independent variation dimensions.','A combination tree grows quickly.|Global recipients create state bugs.|Duplication increases maintenance cost.'],
['M','A fake sender returns queued. What remains unverified?','The real delivery adapter','The local delegation call','The fixture’s returned string','The constructor assignment','The fake replaces external protocol behavior, which needs separate scoped tests.','The local call can be observed.|The fixture returned that string.|The assignment executed locally.'],
['H','A subclass raises unsupported-operation for a method callers require. What must be reviewed?','Substitutability','Only its indentation','The number of imports','The terminal size','A subtype must honor the relevant behavioral contract expected by its clients.','Formatting does not repair the contract.|Import count is unrelated.|Terminal size does not affect substitutability.'],
['H','When is inheritance still a reasonable choice?','A compatible specialization','Whenever two names rhyme','To avoid every constructor','To grant automatic authorization','A genuine specialization can reuse and extend behavior while preserving the base contract.','Naming resemblance is not a semantic relationship.|Construction may still be needed.|Inheritance does not authorize users.'] ]});
L.push({id:'pio-super',comp:'m2.c2',title:'Trace method resolution and cooperative super calls',before:'Understand inheritance as a behavioral relationship and ordinary method dispatch.',intro:'A logging mixin and a base processor both contribute to a workflow. Calling a parent by name can skip another class in a multiple-inheritance chain. super follows the method resolution order for the actual type, so cooperative designs must agree on compatible call signatures.',sections:[['Read the method resolution order','Python records a method resolution order, or MRO, for each class. Attribute/method lookup follows that order. In a simple single-inheritance class, super often reaches the immediate base, but the general meaning is the next implementation in the MRO after the current class.','Inspect the MRO instead of guessing from the leftmost source declaration alone. A diamond hierarchy can be linearized so one shared base appears once. If cooperative methods disagree about arguments or omit super when continuation is required, a valid class definition can still execute the wrong sequence.'],['Keep cooperation explicit','Use super consistently when every participating implementation follows the same cooperative contract. A method can intentionally terminate a chain, but that choice needs a clear design. Prefer composition when independent behaviors do not benefit from a shared cooperative hierarchy.','The fixture traces a small diamond with no external effects. Its list shows the dispatch order, not a universal recommendation for multiple inheritance. Real constructors and mixins need equally careful signature and state design.']],problem:'Observe a cooperative diamond’s method order without double-calling its base.',steps:['Define a shared terminal base.','Let both intermediate implementations call super.','Inspect the combined method output and class MRO.'],code:'class Base:\n    def steps(self): return ["base"]\nclass Audit(Base):\n    def steps(self): return ["audit"] + super().steps()\nclass Validate(Base):\n    def steps(self): return ["validate"] + super().steps()\nclass Processor(Audit, Validate): pass\nprint(Processor().steps())\nprint([c.__name__ for c in Processor.__mro__[:-1]])',expect:"['audit', 'validate', 'base']\n['Processor', 'Audit', 'Validate', 'Base']",result:'super in Audit continues to Validate for this actual type, then Base. It is not simply a fixed call to Audit’s named parent.',mistakes:['super does not universally mean a fixed lexical parent.','Mixing direct base calls with cooperative calls can skip or repeat behavior.'],takeaways:['Inspect the actual MRO when tracing dispatch.','Require compatible signatures and deliberate chain continuation.'],refs:['classes','datamodel'],q:[
['E','What does MRO stand for?','Method resolution order','Mutable resource ownership','Module registration option','Memory reset operation','The MRO defines the class search order used for method and attribute resolution.','Ownership is a different concept.|It is not module registration.|It does not reset memory.'],
['E','In cooperative inheritance, what does super use?','The actual MRO chain','Only the filename order','A random base method','The latest imported module','super continues lookup according to the actual class hierarchy’s resolution order.','Files do not define dispatch order.|Dispatch is deterministic under the hierarchy.|Imports do not replace MRO semantics.'],
['M','Audit and Validate both call super before a shared Base. What can a valid MRO provide?','One ordered shared-base visit','Automatic repeated Base calls','No possible method dispatch','A copy of every class body','Cooperative lookup follows a linearization with the shared base appearing once.','Repeats are not required by the diamond.|Methods can dispatch normally.|Bodies are not copied for each call.'],
['M','Where can a learner inspect a class’s resolution sequence?','Its __mro__ attribute','Only a production database','Only the exception message','Its source filename suffix','The class exposes its linearized MRO for inspection.','No database is required.|An error need not occur.|Filename suffixes do not show resolution.'],
['H','One cooperative method omits super unexpectedly. What can happen?','The chain stops early','Every base still runs automatically','Python repairs the missing call','All objects become identical','Continuation occurs only when the method deliberately invokes the next implementation.','Later methods are not automatic.|Python does not infer the intended call.|Identity is unaffected.'],
['H','Why do cooperative mixins need compatible argument handling?','Calls continue across classes','Every method is encrypted','The MRO validates business inputs','Arguments are discarded always','Each next implementation must accept the contract passed along the chain.','Inheritance is not encryption.|MRO does not validate domain inputs.|Arguments are not automatically discarded.'] ]});
L.push({id:'pio-polymorphism',comp:'m2.c3',title:'Replace repeated type branches with a tested protocol',before:'Understand composition and compatible behavior before abstracting dispatch.',intro:'A fulfilment service repeatedly checks whether an item ships by courier or pickup. If each option can calculate its own delivery fee under a shared contract, the service can ask the object for the fee instead of maintaining an expanding if/elif chain.',sections:[['Program against behavior the caller actually needs','Polymorphism lets different types respond to the same operation in type-appropriate ways. Python can use duck typing: an object is suitable when it supports the required behavior, without forcing one inheritance tree. Type protocols or abstract base classes can document parts of that contract where useful.','Do not move a switch into a method and call the design finished. Keep the operation small and specify units, errors and side effects. Two fee implementations returning different currencies are not compatible merely because both implement fee().'],['Know when a branch is simpler','A short stable decision may be clearer as a conditional. Introduce an interface when behavior varies, needs independent testing or is extended by separate modules. Avoid a large class hierarchy for a single constant mapping.','The fixture uses two fulfilment objects with the same integer minor-unit fee contract. The aggregate dispatches through their methods. It does not charge money or validate delivery eligibility; those remain separate requirements.']],problem:'Calculate two delivery fees through one caller without checking concrete types.',steps:['Define courier and pickup implementations with the same method.','Iterate over objects through the shared operation.','Verify results in declared minor units.'],code:'class Courier:\n    def fee(self, subtotal): return 500 if subtotal < 5000 else 0\nclass Pickup:\n    def fee(self, subtotal): return 0\ndef fees(options, subtotal):\n    return [option.fee(subtotal) for option in options]\nprint(fees([Courier(), Pickup()], 3000))\nprint(fees([Courier(), Pickup()], 6000))',expect:'[500, 0]\n[0, 0]',result:'The caller uses shared behavior while implementations select their own fee rule. Units and eligible inputs remain part of the documented contract.',mistakes:['Matching method names without matching units or error semantics is not sufficient.','Abstracting a trivial stable choice can add complexity without benefit.'],takeaways:['Specify the smallest useful behavioral protocol.','Test each implementation and the common caller’s assumptions.'],refs:['classes','typing'],q:[
['E','What does polymorphic dispatch let a caller do?','Use a shared operation','Read every object’s private files','Avoid every runtime error','Ignore all method contracts','Different implementations can respond to the same required operation.','Polymorphism is not unrestricted access.|Errors remain possible.|Contracts remain essential.'],
['E','What does duck typing emphasize?','Supported behavior','Only exact class names','Only source directory layout','A mandatory single base class','Python callers can work with objects that provide the required operations.','Exact names need not match.|Directories do not define the protocol.|Inheritance is not always necessary.'],
['M','Two fee methods return different currencies without declaring it. What is wrong?','Their contract is inconsistent','Their names are too short','They need the same filename','Polymorphism forbids numbers','A common caller needs compatible units and semantics, not merely a shared spelling.','Name length is irrelevant.|Filenames do not align units.|Numbers are valid results.'],
['M','Which test should every implementation of a fee protocol satisfy?','The shared behavior contract','The same source line count','An identical internal algorithm','A fixed memory address','Implementations can differ internally while honoring required inputs, outputs and failures.','Length is not a behavior rule.|Internal algorithms may vary.|Addresses need not match.'],
['H','When might a simple conditional be preferable to a new hierarchy?','A small stable choice','When contracts are unknowable','When tests are forbidden','When every class is identical','Abstraction should earn its cost; a short stable rule can be clearer directly.','Unknown contracts need clarification.|Tests remain useful.|Identical classes suggest unnecessary design.'],
['H','A caller checks concrete types before invoking each implementation. What extensibility issue may remain?','New types require caller edits','Every implementation is invalid','The program cannot execute','No protocol can be tested','A repeated type switch can keep the caller coupled to every variant.','Existing variants may work.|The code can run.|Protocols can be behavior-tested.'] ]});
L.push({id:'pio-generators',comp:'m3.c1',title:'Process a stream lazily with generators',before:'Understand for loops and functions; the next lesson explains the general iterator protocol.',intro:'An analytics export can contain far more rows than fit comfortably in a list. A generator yields one result at a time, allowing downstream code to consume a bounded prefix or process records incrementally. Laziness changes when computation and failures occur.',sections:[['Yield preserves a suspended computation','Calling a generator function creates a generator object without immediately executing its body. Iteration resumes at yield, returns a value to the consumer and pauses with local state preserved. Once exhausted, that generator does not automatically restart; create another generator when a fresh pass is needed.','A generator expression or function can avoid retaining every produced result. The source may still consume memory: iterating a huge already-loaded list does not make the upstream loading disappear. Measure the complete pipeline rather than assuming yield guarantees constant memory everywhere.'],['Keep resource lifetime aligned with consumption','Lazy code may execute after the function that created it returns. Files, transactions and exception boundaries must remain valid while the consumer iterates. Do not close a file before a returned generator tries to read it, and do not claim that an unconsumed generator validated its inputs.','The fixture uses a tiny synthetic sequence and records which rows are visited. Consuming two values proves that later rows were not processed yet. It is a behavioral demonstration, not a benchmark of real dataset size or external I/O.']],problem:'Consume only two transformed records and observe the limited work performed.',steps:['Create a generator that records each visited input.','Read a bounded prefix with islice.','Inspect the visited list without consuming the rest.'],code:'from itertools import islice\nvisited = []\ndef amounts(rows):\n    for row in rows:\n        visited.append(row)\n        yield row * 10\nstream = amounts([1, 2, 3, 4])\nprint(visited)\nprint(list(islice(stream, 2)))\nprint(visited)',expect:'[]\n[10, 20]\n[1, 2]',result:'No body work occurred on construction, and only two inputs were processed by the bounded consumer. The original fixture list was already in memory.',mistakes:['A generator over an already-loaded list does not undo upstream memory cost.','Lazy exceptions occur during consumption, not necessarily at generator creation.'],takeaways:['Track both source and transformation memory behavior.','Keep resources and error handling valid for the consumption lifetime.'],refs:['classes','datamodel'],q:[
['E','When does a generator function body normally begin executing?','When iteration advances it','Immediately on definition','Only when Python exits','Before its call arguments exist','Calling creates the generator object; advancing it begins body execution.','Definition does not run the body.|Exit is not the iteration trigger.|Arguments are evaluated for the call.'],
['E','What does yield do in a generator?','Produce and suspend','Erase every local variable','Create a new process','Load all future results','Yield returns a value to the consumer while preserving suspended generator state.','Local state is preserved.|No process is required.|Future results need not be loaded.'],
['M','A consumer reads two values from a generator over many rows. What work may remain deferred?','Later row transformations','The two consumed values','The generator’s definition','Every call argument evaluation','Later body iterations occur only when the consumer advances further.','Those values were produced.|The definition already exists.|Call arguments were already evaluated.'],
['M','An exhausted generator is iterated again. What should a fresh full pass normally use?','A newly created generator','The same exhausted object blindly','A renamed loop variable','Only a longer timeout','A consumed generator does not automatically reset its iteration state.','Exhaustion remains.|Names do not reset state.|Time does not restart it.'],
['H','A function returns a generator using a file already closed by its context manager. What is the risk?','Consumption uses a closed resource','The generator copied all bytes automatically','The file reopens itself always','No exception can occur lazily','Lazy work may happen after the resource lifetime ended.','Generators do not necessarily copy source data.|Automatic reopening is not guaranteed.|Exceptions can occur during iteration.'],
['H','Does yield alone guarantee a whole import pipeline uses bounded memory?','No, inspect upstream storage','Yes, every source is streamed','Yes, all lists disappear','Only if the file extension is py','The source or downstream accumulation may still retain large datasets.','Sources can be preloaded.|Existing lists remain.|Extensions do not determine memory behavior.'] ]});
L.push({id:'pio-iterators',comp:'m3.c2',title:'Trace iterables, iterators and exhaustion',before:'Use the generator lesson to distinguish a lazy producer from its consumer.',intro:'A shopping basket list can support repeated traversal, while one iterator remembers how far a single traversal has progressed. Many bugs arise when a service passes an iterator where another component expects a reusable collection.',sections:[['Separate reusable sources from traversal state','An iterable can provide an iterator through iter(). An iterator returns itself from __iter__ and provides successive values through __next__, raising StopIteration when exhausted. A list is iterable and can create independent iterators; one iterator generally represents one progressing traversal.','The for statement obtains an iterator and repeatedly advances it until normal exhaustion. Do not catch every exception as if it were StopIteration: conversion or I/O errors represent failures, not the end of valid data. In generator functions, use return for normal completion rather than manually raising StopIteration.'],['Make consumption ownership visible','Document whether a function accepts any iterable, consumes an iterator once or requires a sequence with length/indexing. Materializing list(source) can provide repeatability, but costs memory and can hang for an infinite source. A second pass should be a deliberate requirement rather than an accidental assumption.','The fixture creates two independent iterators from one list, showing that advancing one does not advance the other. It also uses next with a default to represent expected exhaustion without a broad exception handler.']],problem:'Observe independent traversals and explicit exhaustion.',steps:['Create two iterators from the same reusable list.','Advance one twice and the other once.','Request a final value using an exhaustion default.'],code:'items = ["pen", "book"]\na, b = iter(items), iter(items)\nprint(next(a), next(a), next(b))\nprint(next(a, "finished"))\nprint(iter(b) is b)',expect:'pen book pen\nfinished\nTrue',result:'The iterators have different positions and an iterator returns itself from iter. This does not make arbitrary shared iterators safe for concurrent consumers.',mistakes:['A one-pass iterator should not be assumed reusable like a list.','Catching every exception as end-of-stream hides real failures.'],takeaways:['Distinguish the iterable source from traversal state.','Specify consumption and repeatability in function contracts.'],refs:['classes','datamodel'],q:[
['E','What operation obtains an iterator from an iterable?','iter(value)','str(value) always','len(value) always','del value','iter invokes the iteration protocol to obtain traversal state.','String conversion has another purpose.|Not every iterable has length.|Deletion does not start traversal.'],
['E','What signals normal iterator exhaustion?','StopIteration','Every ValueError','A random None always','A database timeout','The iterator protocol uses StopIteration for completion.','ValueError can indicate a real failure.|None can be valid data.|Timeouts are not normal iteration completion.'],
['M','Two iterators are created from one list. What is normally independent?','Their traversal positions','The list’s type','The Python executable path','Every element’s identity','Each iterator keeps its own progress through the shared iterable.','Both reference the same list type.|Executable paths are unrelated.|Elements need not be copied.'],
['M','A function needs two passes over a one-shot stream. What must be planned?','A replay/storage plan','Only renaming the parameter','An assumption it restarts','Ignoring the second result','Repeatability requires a source that can replay or an explicit storage choice with costs.','Names do not reset consumption.|One-shot streams need not restart.|Discarding required output violates the contract.'],
['H','Why can list(source) be unsafe for an unbounded source?','It may never finish','It always encrypts values','It removes every exception','It guarantees constant memory','Materialization tries to consume all values and can grow indefinitely for an infinite stream.','Lists do not encrypt.|Source failures can propagate.|Accumulation consumes memory.'],
['H','A parser catches any exception and treats it as iterator exhaustion. What can be hidden?','Real data or I/O failures','Only normal completion','The variable spelling','The class definition itself','Only the protocol’s completion signal should end traversal normally; other errors need handling.','Other failures are not normal completion.|Spelling is not the exception boundary.|The definition already exists.'] ]});
L.push({id:'pio-crosscut',comp:'m3.c3',title:'Use context managers and decorators without hiding contracts',before:'Understand functions, exceptions and lazy lifetime before wrapping behavior.',intro:'A report operation needs cleanup even when formatting fails, and several service functions need consistent tracing. Context managers express resource lifetime; decorators wrap callable behavior. Both reduce repetition only when their scope and failure semantics remain clear.',sections:[['Make cleanup exception-safe','A with statement enters a context and guarantees its exit protocol when the body leaves normally or through an exception. contextlib.contextmanager can express setup around yield and cleanup in finally. Do not suppress exceptions accidentally: cleanup and conversion of the failure outcome are separate decisions.','An exception during cleanup can replace the active failure unless handled deliberately. Keep cleanup focused and avoid reporting a failed transaction as success. For generators using a resource, ensure the context encloses actual consumption rather than only generator creation.'],['Preserve callable meaning when decorating','A decorator receives a callable and returns a replacement. The wrapper should forward the relevant arguments, return the underlying result and propagate failures according to a documented policy. functools.wraps preserves useful metadata such as name/docstring; it does not prove semantic equivalence.','The fixture records a deterministic resource lifecycle while a decorated computation fails. The wrapper preserves its name and lets the failure propagate after cleanup. It does not implement distributed tracing or production transactions.']],problem:'Verify cleanup and metadata while a wrapped operation raises an expected error.',steps:['Define a context manager with finally cleanup.','Wrap a function using functools.wraps.','Trigger the error and inspect cleanup and callable name.'],code:'from contextlib import contextmanager\nfrom functools import wraps\nevents = []\n@contextmanager\ndef scope():\n    events.append("open")\n    try: yield\n    finally: events.append("close")\ndef traced(function):\n    @wraps(function)\n    def wrapper(*args, **kwargs):\n        with scope(): return function(*args, **kwargs)\n    return wrapper\n@traced\ndef calculate(): raise ValueError("fixture")\ntry: calculate()\nexcept ValueError: events.append("failed")\nprint(events)\nprint(calculate.__name__)',expect:"['open', 'close', 'failed']\ncalculate",result:'Cleanup occurs before the caller handles the propagated error, and wraps preserves the callable name. No exception was silently converted into success.',mistakes:['A wrapper that forgets return can silently change a function’s result to None.','Suppressing a body exception must be an explicit contract, not an accidental cleanup effect.'],takeaways:['Align context lifetime with actual resource use.','Preserve arguments, results, metadata and intended failures when decorating.'],refs:['contextlib','functools'],q:[
['E','Which statement expresses context-manager lifetime?','with','global only','import only','pass only','with invokes entry and exit behavior around the body’s execution.','Global declarations do not manage lifetime.|Import is module loading.|Pass performs no lifecycle operation.'],
['E','What helps a decorator preserve the wrapped function’s metadata?','functools.wraps','A random new function name','Deleting the docstring','A shared global list','wraps copies useful metadata while the wrapper still owns behavioral correctness.','Renaming alone does not preserve metadata.|Deletion loses information.|A list is not metadata preservation.'],
['M','A wrapper calls the function but omits return. What may change?','The returned result becomes None','The function is never invoked','Every exception is suppressed','All arguments are encrypted','Without returning the underlying result, the wrapper implicitly returns None.','The call can still run.|Exceptions propagate unless handled.|Argument encryption is unrelated.'],
['M','Where should cleanup live in a generator-based context manager?','A finally path around yield','Only after successful use','Only in a comment','In an unrelated module import','A finally block can run when the with body exits through failure as well as success.','Success-only cleanup leaks on failure.|Comments do not execute.|Unrelated imports do not own the resource.'],
['H','A decorator catches every exception and returns success. What contract may be broken?','Failure propagation','Only the function filename','Only local indentation','The existence of with syntax','Cross-cutting wrappers must not silently turn operational failure into successful business results.','Filenames do not define outcomes.|Formatting is not the semantic problem.|Syntax can remain valid while behavior is wrong.'],
['H','A context ends before a lazy iterator consumes its resource. What should change?','Enclose the consumption lifetime','Only lengthen the variable name','Assume yield copied all data','Suppress the resource error','The resource must remain valid while deferred work actually uses it.','Names do not extend lifetime.|Laziness need not copy inputs.|Suppression hides the defect.'] ]});
L.push({id:'pio-exceptions',comp:'m4.c1',title:'Design exception boundaries that preserve meaning',before:'Review exception handling in Python Fundamentals and cleanup in the previous lesson.',intro:'A CSV import can fail because a row is malformed, a file is unavailable or a programming assumption is wrong. Callers need to distinguish these outcomes. A small domain exception can add useful context without catching every failure and returning empty success.',sections:[['Use exception types for actionable distinctions','Create custom exceptions by deriving from an appropriate Exception subclass. Group failures only when callers should handle them similarly. Keep public messages stable and safe, while preserving a cause internally when it helps diagnostics. Do not include raw credentials or complete private records in exception text.','Catch errors at the boundary that can make a meaningful decision: retry, reject the input, clean up, translate or propagate. Catching a broad base class too early can hide unrelated defects. KeyboardInterrupt and SystemExit are not ordinary business-validation errors to swallow in application code.'],['Translate without erasing the cause','raise DomainError(...) from error records an explicit cause. It helps controlled diagnostics connect the domain operation to the underlying failure. The client-facing API can still return a safe category and correlation id rather than the entire traceback.','The fixture converts one known integer parsing error into a domain-specific failure. It does not classify arbitrary I/O errors as malformed input and does not expose real row contents. Tests should assert the stable error contract and relevant valid inputs.']],problem:'Translate one known parsing failure while retaining its internal cause.',steps:['Define a domain exception for quantity format.','Catch only ValueError from the conversion.','Inspect the translated type and retained cause.'],code:'class QuantityFormatError(Exception): pass\ndef quantity(raw):\n    try:\n        return int(raw)\n    except ValueError as error:\n        raise QuantityFormatError("invalid quantity format") from error\ntry:\n    quantity("invalid")\nexcept QuantityFormatError as error:\n    print(type(error).__name__)\n    print(type(error.__cause__).__name__)\nprint(quantity("7"))',expect:'QuantityFormatError\nValueError\n7',result:'The domain failure preserves the parse cause, and valid conversion remains successful. This does not define a public API’s disclosure policy by itself.',mistakes:['Catching every failure as invalid input can hide programming or availability defects.','Exception messages can leak private values if constructed carelessly.'],takeaways:['Use types and boundaries that support a real caller decision.','Retain controlled diagnostic causes without public overdisclosure.'],refs:['errors','traceback'],q:[
['E','What base normally suits an application’s custom recoverable exception?','Exception','A random integer','Only list','A string literal','Custom application exceptions normally derive from Exception or a suitable subclass.','Integers are not exception types.|Lists do not define error hierarchy.|A string is not a modern exception class.'],
['E','What does raise DomainError(...) from error preserve?','An explicit cause','The user’s password securely','Every transaction automatically','A guaranteed retry success','Explicit chaining records the relationship to the originating exception.','Chaining is not secret storage.|Transactions need their own mechanism.|Retries may still fail.'],
['M','Where should a known parse error be translated?','A boundary with useful meaning','Every function indiscriminately','Only the stylesheet loader','An unrelated global catch','Translation should add domain context where the caller can act on it.','Repeated broad translation obscures cause.|Stylesheets are unrelated.|An unrelated catch lacks the right contract.'],
['M','Which exception text is safer for a malformed private row?','A stable format category','The complete row and token','All environment variables','Every neighboring student record','A safe category can describe the failure without unnecessary sensitive payloads.','Private data and tokens should be minimized.|Environment values can contain secrets.|Neighboring records are unnecessary.'],
['H','Why avoid treating all exceptions as ordinary invalid input?','It can hide system defects','Every failure has one cause','Validation should never occur','Python cannot chain errors','Unexpected programming or dependency failures have different meanings from rejected input.','Causes differ.|Validation remains essential.|Python supports explicit chaining.'],
['H','A public API omits private traceback details. What can still support debugging?','Controlled correlated evidence','Publishing raw credentials','Returning success instead','Deleting every error record','Safe request correlation and authorized diagnostics preserve useful evidence without public exposure.','Credentials must remain private.|False success hides failure.|Deleting all evidence impedes repair.'] ]});
L.push({id:'pio-types',comp:'m4.c2',title:'Combine type hints with explicit runtime validation',before:'Understand dataclasses and exceptions; annotations alone are not an input-validation engine.',intro:'An API boundary accepts JSON values, while a service function expects a positive integer quantity. Type hints improve tooling and communication, but untrusted runtime values still need a deliberate contract, including Python’s bool-as-int subclass relationship.',sections:[['Separate static assistance from runtime checks','Annotations describe expected types for readers and static analyzers. The interpreter generally does not enforce a function’s annotations on each call. Static checks can catch mismatched local usage but depend on coverage, configuration and information available at analysis time.','Runtime validation belongs at untrusted input and state boundaries. Check required fields, types, ranges and semantic rules before constructing a trusted model. Avoid accepting a value merely because conversion happened to succeed when the public schema requires a specific representation.'],['Define coercion and edge cases deliberately','isinstance(True, int) is true in Python because bool subclasses int. If an API requires an integer number of items but rejects Boolean values, use an explicit check that reflects that rule. Decide whether numeric strings or fractional numbers may be coerced and report validation failures safely.','The fixture requires an exact integer type and a positive range. It demonstrates one narrow schema, not a universal recommendation to reject subclasses throughout an application. A framework validation library can help, but its actual coercion/default behavior must still be tested.']],problem:'Reject Boolean and string quantities under an exact-positive-integer fixture schema.',steps:['State exact type and positivity requirements.','Validate before returning trusted data.','Compare one valid and three invalid representations.'],code:'def validate_quantity(value: int) -> int:\n    if type(value) is not int or value <= 0:\n        raise ValueError("positive integer required")\n    return value\nfor value in (3, True, "3", 0):\n    try:\n        print(validate_quantity(value))\n    except ValueError:\n        print("rejected")',expect:'3\nrejected\nrejected\nrejected',result:'Only the declared positive integer representation passes. The annotation alone did not perform these checks.',mistakes:['A type annotation is not automatic runtime enforcement.','Implicit coercion can accept representations the API did not intend.'],takeaways:['Use static checks and runtime schemas for their different scopes.','Specify types, coercion and semantic ranges explicitly.'],refs:['typing','datamodel'],q:[
['E','Does a Python function annotation normally reject a wrong runtime argument by itself?','No, checks are separate','Yes, for every function','Only if the name is long','Only after adding a docstring','Annotations usually document types without automatic per-call enforcement.','General enforcement requires additional mechanisms.|Name length is unrelated.|Docstrings do not validate values.'],
['E','Why can isinstance(True, int) return True?','bool subclasses int','True is a string','All objects are integers','Annotations changed the value','Python defines bool as a subclass of int.','True is a Boolean value.|Objects have many types.|Annotations do not perform this conversion.'],
['M','A JSON schema rejects Boolean quantities. Which fixture check can express exact int behavior?','type(value) is int','bool(value) always','str(value) is not empty','value is not None only','An exact-type check excludes Boolean under this explicitly narrow contract.','Truthy Booleans would pass.|String conversion loses representation distinctions.|Non-None values include many invalid types.'],
['M','What should accompany a quantity type check?','The semantic range','Only the variable color','Only the filename','An assumption all integers are valid','A correct type can still hold zero or negative values outside the domain rule.','Colors are irrelevant.|Filenames do not constrain data.|Integer membership alone is insufficient.'],
['H','A validation library silently converts numeric strings. What must the API team decide?','Whether coercion is allowed','Whether Python can use strings','Only the import order','That validation needs no tests','Accepted representations and coercion are part of the public input contract.','Python supports strings regardless.|Import order does not choose policy.|Actual behavior needs testing.'],
['H','A static analyzer passes but live JSON violates a business range. What was missing?','Runtime boundary validation','A longer type alias only','A different text editor','A claim all tests passed','Static type information does not replace runtime validation of untrusted values and semantic rules.','Alias length does not enforce range.|Editors do not validate live requests by themselves.|Claims are not checks.'] ]});
L.push({id:'pio-environments',comp:'m4.c3',title:'Make dependency environments reproducible and reviewable',before:'Understand module imports and the distinction between application source and runtime configuration.',intro:'Two engineers run the same project but install different package versions. An isolated environment reduces accidental package sharing, while explicit dependency records make differences reviewable. Neither isolation nor pinning proves a package is safe or a build is reproducible on every platform.',sections:[['Use virtual environments for Python package scope','A virtual environment supplies a Python environment with its own package installation location and configuration. Activation primarily changes shell command lookup; using the environment’s interpreter path directly is also valid. It is not an operating-system security sandbox and does not isolate filesystem or network access.','Create environments from a declared Python version and document how dependencies are installed. Keep generated environment directories out of source control. Recreate them from source/dependency metadata rather than relying on one developer’s mutable machine state.'],['Pin versions and verify the whole environment','A pinned requirement constrains a package version, but transitive dependencies, platform wheels, interpreter versions, indexes and build tools can still affect results. Hash verification or a reviewed lock workflow can strengthen artifact identity where supported. A clean install and relevant tests provide evidence of the chosen combination.','Update dependencies deliberately for fixes and compatibility; never treat a permanent pin as freedom from vulnerabilities. The fixture creates a disposable venv with an interpreter symlink and without pip or network installation and inspects its local configuration. It does not resolve third-party packages or establish supply-chain safety.']],problem:'Create and inspect an isolated package environment without downloading dependencies.',steps:['Create a temporary environment with venv and no pip bootstrap.','Read the generated configuration.','Verify that system-site packages are disabled and the directory is temporary.'],code:'import tempfile, venv\nfrom pathlib import Path\nwith tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as directory:\n    target = Path(directory) / "environment"\n    venv.EnvBuilder(with_pip=False, symlinks=True).create(target)\n    config = (target / "pyvenv.cfg").read_text()\n    print("include-system-site-packages = false" in config)\n    print((target / "bin" / "python").exists())\nprint(target.exists())',expect:'True\nTrue\nFalse',result:'The temporary venv has its own interpreter entry and excludes system-site packages by default. No package download, vulnerability scan or cross-platform lock verification ran.',mistakes:['Activation changes lookup; it is not a security sandbox.','Pinned versions can still contain vulnerabilities and platform-dependent artifacts.'],takeaways:['Record interpreter and dependency assumptions together.','Recreate and test environments rather than commit generated directories.'],refs:['venv','modules'],q:[
['E','What does a virtual environment mainly isolate?','Python package installation scope','Every operating-system permission','All network requests','Every production database','A venv separates Python package/interpreter configuration, not the whole operating system.','It is not a permission sandbox.|Network access remains possible.|Databases are separate systems.'],
['E','Must a venv be activated to use its interpreter?','No, its path can be invoked','Yes, no path exists','Only after committing the directory','Only on a public server','Activation changes shell lookup; direct interpreter invocation is also valid.','The environment provides an entry path.|Generated directories need not be committed.|Local environments are supported.'],
['M','What should normally be committed instead of a generated venv directory?','Dependency and setup metadata','Every installed cache file','A production secret file','A copy of all interpreter binaries','Recreation instructions and dependency records are more portable and reviewable than a machine-specific environment tree.','Caches are generated artifacts.|Secrets must remain controlled.|Binary copies do not express the dependency contract.'],
['M','What does an exact package version pin establish?','A version constraint','Absence of vulnerabilities','Identical artifacts everywhere','Automatic human review','A pin selects a version but does not by itself establish safety or all platform/build inputs.','Pinned releases can be vulnerable.|Platforms and indexes can differ.|Review remains separate.'],
['H','Direct versions match but installations differ across machines. What else should be inspected?','Transitive and platform inputs','Only the folder color','Only source comment length','The spelling of the author name','Interpreter, transitive dependencies, artifacts and build tooling can affect the resolved environment.','Color is irrelevant.|Comments do not resolve packages.|Author spelling does not determine artifacts.'],
['H','A dependency is permanently pinned and never reviewed. What risk remains?','Unaddressed security fixes','Guaranteed supply-chain safety','Automatic compatibility forever','No need for isolated tests','Pins support control but require deliberate updates, review and validation over time.','Pinning is not a security proof.|Future compatibility is not guaranteed.|Tests remain useful.'] ]});
